## ⚠️ V5.31.0 — THE NBM CONSENSUS ANCHOR (deployed 2026-08-29)

### What was wrong

**The bracket ladder was built entirely from NBM percentiles. Consensus never
reached bet selection.** `bracket_probs_nbm()` passed `consensus` in, but it was
only used when `nbm_bracket_prob()` returned None for an individual bracket.

So every forecasting improvement — the NWS/GFS blend, per-city warm offsets,
bias correction, the V5.30 trimmed mean — moved a number that was logged,
displayed, and stored in `settlements.consensus` and **never affected which
bracket got bet.** NBM alone picked it.

### The measurement (kalshi_snapshots ⋈ settlements, 72 days, 20 cities)

The model's chosen bracket midpoint sat **below its own consensus in 18 of 20
cities**:

| city | bracket_mid − consensus |
|---|---|
| Los Angeles | −2.34 |
| Washington DC | −1.93 |
| Atlanta | −1.89 |
| Denver | −1.76 |
| Philadelphia | −1.42 |
| Phoenix | −1.41 |
| Austin | −1.41 |
| ... | ... |
| New Orleans | +0.12 |
| Dallas | +0.21 |

Worst-offset cities were the same as worst bracket-vs-actual cities — near
one-to-one, so the offset explains most of the cold bias.

And it got **worse** after V5.30.0: pre −0.83 (n=1,926), post −1.48 (n=289),
with 16 of 18 cities moving more negative including several that had been fine
(Chicago +0.12 → −2.65, Vegas −0.09 → −1.64).

### Ruled out before landing on the answer

- **BASE_SIGMA** — no correlation. Boston (σ3.5) offsets −1.00; Phoenix (σ0.9)
  offsets −1.41. Sigma only affects the fallback path.
- **CITY_PREDICTION_MODE** — both modes in both groups.
- **NWS_BIAS_BOOST_CITIES** — San Antonio is in the set at −0.17; the two worst
  cities (LA, Atlanta) are not in it.

### The fix

`nbm_bracket_prob()` takes an `anchor` and shifts every percentile point so p50
lands on consensus. **NBM supplies the SHAPE, consensus supplies the CENTER.**

`NBM_CONSENSUS_ANCHOR = 1.0` in BOTH files. **The two values must match** or the
app and cron pick different brackets. Set to 0.0 to revert exactly to V5.30 —
that is the entire rollback procedure.

### ⚠️ The honest caveat — now being measured (V5.31.1)

We measured that the bracket sits below consensus, and that consensus is
calibrated. We **never measured NBM p50 against actual** — NBM percentiles were
fetched live and thrown away, so there was no history to check. "NBM runs cold"
was an INFERENCE from the offset, not an observation. If NBM is right and
consensus is wrong on the days that matter, this change makes things worse.

**V5.31.1 (2026-08-30) closes that hole.** `settlements.nbm_p50` now stores the
value, so the premise becomes checkable in ~10 days instead of remaining an
assumption underneath a change that affects every bet. See CHECKPOINTS.

Implementation notes:
- `ALTER TABLE public.settlements ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS nbm_p50 NUMERIC(6,2);`
- Storage only. Nothing reads the column — no ladder math touched, so
  `streamlit_app.py` at V5.31.0 and `fetch_weather.py` at V5.31.1 still pick
  identical brackets. **The validation window is NOT reset by this.**
- NBM is fetched only during firing windows, so `nbm_p50` is NULL on other runs.
  ~5 readings per city-day, which is plenty.
- On an existing row `nbm_p50` is only overwritten when non-null, so a later
  non-window run cannot blank out a p50 an earlier window run stored. This was a
  real bug risk, not a theoretical one — `sb_upsert` runs on every pass.

### Aside: "didn't NBM already fail?"

NBM was tried and dropped in the **MANUAL research** — as a standalone forecast
read against the market. **That says little about its use here.** In the model
NBM supplies the SHAPE of a probability distribution across brackets, and as of
V5.31 it no longer sets the center at all. Different question. But it does raise
the value of the `nbm_p50` measurement: if NBM's central estimate was poor in
manual work, we want to know whether it is poor here too.

